This example assumes you've already extracted the IPv4 payload containing the TCP segment from your Uint8Array.

```typescript
interface TCPHeader {
  sourcePort: number;
  destinationPort: number;
  sequenceNumber: number;
  acknowledgmentNumber: number;
  dataOffset: number;
  reserved: number;
  flags: {
    ns: boolean;
    cwr: boolean;
    ece: boolean;
    urg: boolean;
    ack: boolean;
    psh: boolean;
    rst: boolean;
    syn: boolean;
    fin: boolean;
  };
  windowSize: number;
  checksum: number;
  urgentPointer: number;
  options?: Uint8Array; // For optional TCP options
  data?: Uint8Array; // TCP payload data
}

function parseTCPHeader(tcpSegment: Uint8Array): TCPHeader | null {
  // Minimum TCP header length is 20 bytes
  if (tcpSegment.length < 20) {
    return null;
  }

  const sourcePort = tcpSegment[0] * 256 + tcpSegment[1];
  const destinationPort = tcpSegment[2] * 256 + tcpSegment[3];
  const sequenceNumber = (tcpSegment[4] * 256 * 256 * 256 + tcpSegment[5] * 256 * 256 + tcpSegment[6] * 256 + tcpSegment[7]);
  const acknowledgmentNumber = (tcpSegment[8] * 256 * 256 * 256 + tcpSegment[9] * 256 * 256 + tcpSegment[10] * 256 + tcpSegment[11]);
  const dataOffset = (tcpSegment[12] >> 4) * 4; // Data offset in bytes
  const reserved = (tcpSegment[12] & 0x0f); // Reserved bits
  const flags = {
    ns: (tcpSegment[13] & 0x01) !== 0,
    cwr: (tcpSegment[13] & 0x80) !== 0,
    ece: (tcpSegment[13] & 0x40) !== 0,
    urg: (tcpSegment[13] & 0x20) !== 0,
    ack: (tcpSegment[13] & 0x10) !== 0,
    psh: (tcpSegment[13] & 0x08) !== 0,
    rst: (tcpSegment[13] & 0x04) !== 0,
    syn: (tcpSegment[13] & 0x02) !== 0,
    fin: (tcpSegment[13] & 0x01) !== 0,
  };
  const windowSize = tcpSegment[14] * 256 + tcpSegment[15];
  const checksum = tcpSegment[16] * 256 + tcpSegment[17];
  const urgentPointer = tcpSegment[18] * 256 + tcpSegment[19];

  let options: Uint8Array | undefined;
  let data: Uint8Array | undefined;

  if (dataOffset > 20) {
    options = tcpSegment.subarray(20, dataOffset);
    data = tcpSegment.subarray(dataOffset);
  } else {
    data = tcpSegment.subarray(20);
  }

  return {
    sourcePort,
    destinationPort,
    sequenceNumber,
    acknowledgmentNumber,
    dataOffset,
    reserved,
    flags,
    windowSize,
    checksum,
    urgentPointer,
    options,
    data
  };
}


// Example usage (replace with your actual Uint8Array)
const sampleTCPSegment = new Uint8Array([
  0x00, 0x50, 0x00, 0x35, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x50, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x48, 0x65, 0x6c, 0x6c, 0x6f
]);

const tcpHeader = parseTCPHeader(sampleTCPSegment);
console.log(tcpHeader);
```

Remember: This is a *very* simplified parser.  It lacks error handling, checksum verification, and doesn't handle all possible TCP options.  A production-ready TCP implementation requires significantly more effort and would likely use a networking library.  This example is for educational purposes to demonstrate the basic parsing of a TCP header.  Consider using a library like `ws` (for WebSocket) if you need TCP functionality in a browser environment or a Node.js networking library for server-side applications.

You can't directly use `nix-shell -p` with packages from `nixpkgs-unstable`.  `nix-shell -p` expects packages from the currently configured channels (usually `nixpkgs` stable).  `nixpkgs-unstable` is a separate channel, and you need to explicitly specify it.

Here are a few ways to achieve this:

**1. Using `nix-shell` with `-I` and the `pkgs` override:**

This is the cleanest and recommended approach. It allows you to keep your stable `nixpkgs` configuration while temporarily accessing the unstable version for specific packages.

```bash
nix-shell -I nixpkgs=https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/archive/master.tar.gz -p <package-name>
```

Replace `<package-name>` with the actual package name from `nixpkgs-unstable`.  This command overlays the `nixpkgs` channel with the unstable branch.  Only the specified package will be pulled from unstable; other packages will still come from your stable channel.  This is crucial for avoiding potential conflicts. Note that `master` is a moving target and you might want to use a specific commit hash for reproducibility instead.

**2.  Using a temporary `shell.nix` file:**

This approach is useful for more complex scenarios involving multiple packages or dependencies from `nixpkgs-unstable`.

Create a file named `shell.nix` with the following content:

```nix
let
  pkgs = import <nixpkgs> {
    config = {
      packageOverrides = pkgs: {
        # Override only the packages you need from unstable
        <package-name> = pkgs.callPackage ./<path-to-unstable-package-definition> {};
      };
    };
  };
in pkgs.mkShell {
  buildInputs = [ pkgs.<package-name> ];
}
```

* Replace `<package-name>` with the package name.
* Replace `<path-to-unstable-package-definition>` with the path to the relevant package definition within the unstable branch (you'll need to clone the nixpkgs repository for this).  This is generally not recommended unless you are familiar with the structure of nixpkgs.

Then, run:

```bash
nix-shell
```

This will build and enter a shell environment with the specified package from `nixpkgs-unstable`.

**3. Switching your nixpkgs channel (Least Recommended):**

You can temporarily change your system's `nixpkgs` channel to point to `nixpkgs-unstable`, but this is generally not advised because it affects your entire system and can lead to instability.  Only do this if you fully understand the implications and are prepared to handle potential problems.  You would typically modify your `configuration.nix` or use a similar mechanism to switch channels.  This method is not detailed here due to the risk involved.


**Important Considerations:**

* **Reproducibility:**  Using `master` (or any branch) of `nixpkgs-unstable` means your environment might change as the branch is updated.  For reproducible builds, always use a specific commit hash instead of a branch name.  You can find the commit hashes on GitHub.
* **Dependencies:**  Packages from `nixpkgs-unstable` might have dependencies that aren't available in your stable channel.  Pay close attention to any error messages you encounter.
* **Stability:**  `nixpkgs-unstable` is, by definition, unstable.  Expect potential breakage and be prepared to troubleshoot issues.


The first method using `-I` is generally the safest and most practical approach for most users who only need a single package from the unstable channel.  The second method is preferable when working with multiple packages or more complex dependencies.  Avoid the third method unless you have a strong reason and understand the risks. Remember to always back up your system before making significant changes to your Nix configuration.

