This program reads URLs from a file, separates YouTube URLs from others, and downloads the YouTube URLs using `yt-dlp`.  It handles errors gracefully and provides informative output.  You'll need to install `yt-dlp` and the `regex` crate (add `regex = "1"` to your `Cargo.toml` file).

```rust
use regex::Regex;
use std::error::Error;
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader};
use std::process::{Command, Stdio};

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    let url_file_path = "urls.txt"; // Replace with your file path

    let file = File::open(url_file_path)?;
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);

    let youtube_url_regex = Regex::new(r"^(https?://)?(www\.)?(youtube\.com|youtu\.be)/.*$")?;

    let mut youtube_urls: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
    let mut other_urls: Vec<String> = Vec::new();

    for line in reader.lines() {
        let url = line?;
        let url = url.trim();
        if url.is_empty() {
            continue;
        }

        if youtube_url_regex.is_match(url) {
            youtube_urls.push(url.to_string());
        } else {
            other_urls.push(url.to_string());
        }
    }

    println!("YouTube URLs:");
    for url in &youtube_urls {
        println!("{}", url);
        download_youtube_video(url)?;
    }

    println!("\nOther URLs:");
    for url in &other_urls {
        println!("{}", url);
    }

    Ok(())
}

fn download_youtube_video(url: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    println!("Downloading: {}", url);
    let output = Command::new("yt-dlp")
        .arg(url)
        .stdout(Stdio::piped())
        .stderr(Stdio::piped())
        .output()?;

    if output.status.success() {
        println!("Download successful!");
        let stdout = String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stdout);
        println!("yt-dlp output:\n{}", stdout);
    } else {
        let stderr = String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stderr);
        return Err(format!("Download failed: {}", stderr).into());
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

**To use this code:**

1. **Create a file named `urls.txt`** in the same directory as your Rust program.  Put one URL per line in this file.  For example:

```
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ
https://www.example.com
https://youtu.be/jNQXAC9IVRw
https://www.google.com
```

2. **Install `yt-dlp`:**  Use your system's package manager (e.g., `apt install yt-dlp` on Debian/Ubuntu, `brew install yt-dlp` on macOS with Homebrew) or download it from the official website.

3. **Install the `regex` crate:** Add `regex = "1"` to the dependencies section of your `Cargo.toml` file.

4. **Compile and run the Rust program:**  Use `cargo run`.


