There are several ways to extract hashtags from text in C#. Here are a few examples, ranging from simple to more robust solutions:

**Method 1: Simple Regular Expression**

This method uses a regular expression to find all strings starting with `#`.  It's simple but might not handle edge cases perfectly (e.g., hashtags within URLs).

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class HashtagExtractor
{
    public static List<string> ExtractHashtags(string text)
    {
        List<string> hashtags = new List<string>();
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(text, "#\\w+"); // Matches # followed by one or more word characters

        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            hashtags.Add(match.Value);
        }

        return hashtags;
    }

    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string text = "This is a #test tweet with #multiple #hashtags and some other text.  #ThisIsALongerHashtag";
        List<string> extractedHashtags = ExtractHashtags(text);

        Console.WriteLine("Extracted Hashtags:");
        foreach (string hashtag in extractedHashtags)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(hashtag);
        }
    }
}
```

**Method 2: More Robust Regular Expression**

This improves upon the first method by handling Unicode characters and being slightly more precise in what it considers a hashtag.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class HashtagExtractor
{
    public static List<string> ExtractHashtags(string text)
    {
        List<string> hashtags = new List<string>();
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(text, @"#\p{L}\w*"); // Matches # followed by a letter and then zero or more word characters

        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            hashtags.Add(match.Value);
        }

        return hashtags;
    }

    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string text = "This is a #test tweet with #multiple #hashtags and some other text. #ThisIsALongerHashtag #你好世界";
        List<string> extractedHashtags = ExtractHashtags(text);

        Console.WriteLine("Extracted Hashtags:");
        foreach (string hashtag in extractedHashtags)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(hashtag);
        }
    }
}
```

**Method 3:  Splitting and Filtering (Simpler, less robust)**

This approach is simpler to understand but less robust than regular expressions, particularly if your input text has unusual formatting.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class HashtagExtractor
{
    public static List<string> ExtractHashtags(string text)
    {
        return text.Split(' ')
                   .Where(word => word.StartsWith("#"))
                   .ToList();
    }

    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string text = "This is a #test tweet with #multiple #hashtags and some other text.";
        List<string> extractedHashtags = ExtractHashtags(text);

        Console.WriteLine("Extracted Hashtags:");
        foreach (string hashtag in extractedHashtags)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(hashtag);
        }
    }
}
```

Remember to choose the method that best suits your needs and the complexity of the text you're processing.  For most cases, the regular expression approaches (especially Method 2) offer a good balance of simplicity and robustness.  If you need to handle extremely complex scenarios (e.g., nested hashtags or hashtags within quotes), a more sophisticated parsing technique might be necessary.

